Morocco Water Technology Laboratory (MWTL): Implementation-Ready Design

Author: Ouadi Maakoul
Date: September 2026

AI: chatGpt, Deepseek, Gemini,Grok XAi.

Abstract

The Morocco Water Technology Laboratory (MWTL) is a bilateral research, experimentation, and industrialization framework designed to validate water-efficiency technologies under real agronomic and regulatory conditions. This document presents the final implementation-ready design. It corrects the unit economics of the 50-hectare citrus pilot in Souss-Massa, introduces a statistically powered randomized experimental protocol, distinguishes cash savings from economic water value, expands operating expenditure to realistic levels, and provides a risk register. The corrected base case yields a net annual cash flow of 751,250 MAD, a simple payback of 1.30 years (15.7 months) with subsidy and 1.86 years (22.4 months) without, a 5-year net present value of 2,019,524 MAD at 8%, and an internal rate of return of approximately 71.5%. The sensitivity analysis confirms that the investment case strengthens as water cost rises and as the fraction of conserved water that translates into cash savings approaches unity. The central proposition remains: build one system, measure it rigorously, compare it with the baseline, and determine whether it creates real economic value.

---

1. Introduction

Morocco’s structural water deficit, at approximately 600 m³ per capita per year, remains an existential constraint on agricultural sustainability and industrial competitiveness. The Morocco Water Technology Laboratory (MWTL) is a bilateral research, experimentation, and industrialization framework designed to validate water-efficiency technologies under real agronomic and regulatory conditions. The objective is not to expand the concept but to make the first pilot executable and scientifically defensible. This document provides the complete design: theoretical framework, institutional integration, geographic scope, experimental protocol, unit economics, financing architecture, industrialization pathway, falsification criteria, and risk register.

---

2. Theoretical Framework and Hypotheses

2.1 Primary Hypothesis

Let W denote water consumed in cubic metres, and let V denote economic value generated in MAD. The central research question is whether there exists a technological intervention T such that

\frac{V(T)}{W(T)} > \frac{V(\text{baseline})}{W(\text{baseline})}

subject to environmental, social, and financial constraints. The economic value per cubic metre is defined as

\text{EV/m}^3 = \frac{\Delta V}{\Delta W}

where

\Delta V = V_{\text{intervention}} - V_{\text{baseline}},
\qquad
\Delta W = W_{\text{baseline}} - W_{\text{intervention}}.

2.2 Subsidiary Hypotheses

Hypothesis Proposition Measurable Indicator
H1 High-resolution monitoring identifies inefficiencies undetectable by conventional measurement m³/ha reduction; distribution loss %
H2 Combined environmental, soil, weather, and crop data improves irrigation decisions Prediction accuracy; irrigation efficiency %
H3 Automated irrigation reduces unnecessary consumption while maintaining productivity Tonnes/ha; m³/kg output
H4 Optimized irrigation reduces pumping energy consumption kWh/m³; kWh/ha
H5 Water and energy savings exceed monitoring and control costs Payback period; IRR
H6 Successful systems transition from imported components to Moroccan manufacturing % locally manufactured components; Moroccan engineering hours
H7 Validated systems are adaptable to other African agricultural environments Replication cost; adaptation time

2.3 Statistical Power and Experimental Size

For a two-sample comparison of means, the required number of plots per treatment is

n = \frac{2(Z_{\alpha/2} + Z_\beta)^2 \sigma^2}{\Delta^2},

where \sigma is the coefficient of variation, \Delta is the minimum detectable difference, \alpha is the significance level, and 1-\beta is power.

Using \sigma = 0.15, \Delta = 0.20, \alpha = 0.05, and power = 0.80,

n = \frac{2(1.96 + 0.84)^2 (0.15)^2}{(0.20)^2} \approx 9.

Thus, 10 control and 10 intervention plots are minimally sufficient under the assumed CV. The sensitivity of n to \sigma is material:

\sigma Required n per treatment
0.15 9
0.20 16
0.25 25

If local CV exceeds 0.16, the 10/10 design is underpowered. The pilot therefore uses 10/10 as the minimum, with a pre-specified expansion to 12/12 or 16/16 if the first season’s CV exceeds 0.16. Repeated measures across two or three seasons can also increase effective power. The 50-hectare site is divided into 20 plots of 2.5 hectares. Ten plots are randomly assigned to the control group and ten to the intervention group. Randomization is stratified by soil type and proximity to the water source. Plots must be hydraulically independent; if they share valves, pumps, or pressure zones, buffer plots or a revised layout are required.

2.4 Primary and Secondary Endpoints

Primary endpoint: water consumption (m³/ha). This is the endpoint against which the primary hypothesis is tested.

Secondary endpoints: yield (tonnes/ha), energy consumption (kWh/ha), economic value (MAD/ha), soil moisture (volumetric water content), soil salinity (electrical conductivity), fruit quality (packout percentage, sugar content), maintenance costs, and sensor failure rate.

Multiple secondary endpoints will be analysed with a pre-specified false discovery rate correction, such as Benjamini–Hochberg.

2.5 Measurement Frequency

· Water flow: continuous (15-minute intervals)
· Soil moisture: hourly at three depths
· Weather: continuous
· Yield: at harvest
· Energy: continuous

2.6 Data Management and Analysis Plan

Data shall be stored on a secure cloud platform with role-based access. An independent scientific committee shall have unrestricted access to raw data for validation and publication.

The pre-specified analysis plan is:

1. Intention-to-treat: all randomized plots are analysed in their assigned group.
2. Primary model: ANCOVA of season-average water consumption on treatment, with baseline water use, soil type, and proximity to water source as covariates.
3. Repeated measures: linear mixed-effects models with plot as a random effect and season as a repeated factor.
4. Missing data: multiple imputation or likelihood-based methods under a missing-at-random assumption; sensitivity analysis under missing-not-at-random.
5. Multiple testing: Benjamini–Hochberg for secondary endpoints.
6. Effect size: report standardized mean differences with 95% confidence intervals.

