4. Geographic and Agronomic Scope

The first pilot is located in Souss-Massa, where the Chtouka-Aït Baha desalination plant provides water at a measurable marginal cost. The 50-hectare site is divided into 20 plots of 2.5 hectares. The crop model is export-grade clementines and oranges.

Baseline annual water requirement is 7{,}000 m³/ha, totaling 350{,}000 m³ for the site. The weighted water cost is assumed to be 10 MAD/m³. This figure must be verified against the actual Chtouka irrigation tariff and aquifer pumping costs. If the tariff is not volumetric, the cash-savings case weakens, and the economic value of conserved water must be evaluated at its shadow price.

Pumping energy is assumed at 1.20 MAD/kWh. The baseline annual pumping cost is 105{,}000 MAD. This is consistent with a pumping energy intensity of

\frac{105{,}000}{1.20} = 87{,}500 \text{ kWh/year},

or

\frac{87{,}500}{350{,}000} = 0.25 \text{ kWh/m}^3.

For a pump head H, efficiency \eta, and volume V,

E = \frac{\rho g H V}{3.6 \times 10^6 \eta}.

With H = 50 m, \eta = 0.70, and V = 350{,}000 m³,

E \approx 68{,}125 \text{ kWh},

or 0.195 kWh/m³. The assumed 105{,}000 MAD implies either a higher head or lower efficiency, both plausible. The energy baseline must be measured independently of water volume to avoid double-counting if the water tariff already embeds energy.

Tadla and Drâa-Tafilalet are reserved for subsequent replication, providing contrasts in water source (gravity-fed canals and hyper-arid groundwater, respectively) and crop type (sugar beet, cereals, and date palms).

