Appendix B: Pumping Energy Derivation

B.1 Theoretical Pumping Energy

The hydraulic power required to lift water is

P_h = \rho g Q H,

where \rho = 1000 kg/m³ is water density, g = 9.81 m/s² is gravitational acceleration, Q is flow rate in m³/s, and H is total head in metres.

The electrical power drawn is

P_e = \frac{P_h}{\eta},

where \eta is the pump efficiency (wire-to-water).

The energy per cubic metre is

e = \frac{P_e}{Q} = \frac{\rho g H}{3.6 \times 10^6 \eta} \text{ kWh/m}^3,

where 3.6 \times 10^6 is the conversion factor from joules to kilowatt-hours.

B.2 Application to Souss-Massa Citrus

For a total dynamic head H = 50 m and pump efficiency \eta = 0.70:

e = \frac{1000 \times 9.81 \times 50}{3.6 \times 10^6 \times 0.70}
= \frac{490{,}500}{2{,}520{,}000}
= 0.195 \text{ kWh/m}^3.

For 350,000 m³/year:

E = 0.195 \times 350{,}000 = 68{,}250 \text{ kWh/year}.

At 1.20 MAD/kWh:

\text{Energy cost} = 68{,}250 \times 1.20 = 81{,}900 \text{ MAD/year}.

B.3 Reconciliation with the Base-Case Figure

The base case assumes 105,000 MAD/year, implying

\frac{105{,}000}{1.20} = 87{,}500 \text{ kWh/year}

or

\frac{87{,}500}{350{,}000} = 0.25 \text{ kWh/m}^3.

This corresponds to either:

· a higher total dynamic head: H = 50 \times \frac{0.25}{0.195} \approx 64 m at \eta = 0.70; or
· a lower efficiency: \eta = 0.70 \times \frac{0.195}{0.25} \approx 0.55 at H = 50 m; or
· a combination of both.

Field evidence from Morocco supports the higher range. A study of the Berrechid plain found that groundwater-fed pressurized drip irrigation systems consumed between 4.62 and 4.88 kWh/m³ system-wide, with recycled car engines consuming on average 2.5 kWh/m³. These figures reflect deeper boreholes (up to 120 m) and inefficient pumps. For Souss-Massa, with borehole depths typically ranging from 60 to 150 m and pump efficiencies between 50% and 70%, an energy intensity of 0.25 kWh/m³ is conservative and realistic.

B.4 Energy Savings from Water Savings

If water savings are \Delta W = 87{,}500 m³/year and energy intensity is e = 0.25 kWh/m³:

\Delta E = 87{,}500 \times 0.25 = 21{,}875 \text{ kWh/year}.

At 1.20 MAD/kWh:

\text{Energy savings} = 21{,}875 \times 1.20 = 26{,}250 \text{ MAD/year}.

This is the figure used in the base case. It assumes that the energy savings are proportional to water savings, which holds if the pump operates at the same head and efficiency. If the pump is oversized or operates at variable frequency, the savings may differ. The energy baseline must be measured independently of water volume to avoid double-counting.

