Appendix C: Water Savings and Economic Value Derivation

C.1 Baseline Water Consumption

Citrus water requirements in Souss-Massa are well documented. A study of a commercial clementine orchard in the region found seasonal actual evapotranspiration (ETa) values of 1.68, 3.02, and 1.86 mm/day for initial, mid-, and end-season, respectively. The seasonal actual crop coefficient (Kc act) values were 0.64, 0.58, and 0.64.

The total seasonal water requirement can be estimated as

\text{ET}_{\text{season}} = \sum_{i} \text{ETa}_i \times \Delta t_i,

where \Delta t_i is the duration of each growth stage. For a typical Souss-Massa citrus season:

Stage Duration (days) ETa (mm/day) Total (mm)
Initial 60 1.68 100.8
Mid-season 120 3.02 362.4
End-season 60 1.86 111.6
Total 240 — 574.8

The total crop evapotranspiration is approximately 575 mm (5,750 m³/ha). However, the farmer applies more water than the crop requires. The same study found that 52% of total water supplied was lost through deep percolation, with a deep percolation rate of 813 mm/year. This implies total water supplied:

W_{\text{supplied}} = \frac{\text{ET}}{1 - \text{DPR}} = \frac{575}{1 - 0.52} = \frac{575}{0.48} \approx 1{,}198 \text{ mm} \approx 11{,}980 \text{ m}^3/\text{ha}.

This is consistent with the search result indicating net water requirements of 12,000 to 17,000 m³/ha/year in the region. The base case of 7,000 m³/ha is conservative and assumes improved but not optimized irrigation management.

C.2 Water Savings Potential

The study by Abou Ali et al. (2023) found that "around 55% of the water supply may be saved" with a tailor-made drip irrigation schedule. This is consistent with the 52% deep percolation loss.

The base case assumes 25% savings, which is conservative relative to the 55% theoretical maximum. At 25% savings on 7,000 m³/ha:

\Delta W_{\text{per ha}} = 7{,}000 \times 0.25 = 1{,}750 \text{ m}^3/\text{ha}.

For 50 ha:

\Delta W = 1{,}750 \times 50 = 87{,}500 \text{ m}^3/\text{year}.

C.3 Economic Value of Water

The weighted water cost of 10 MAD/m³ reflects a blend of desalinated water and overexploited aquifer water. The Chtouka-Aït Baha desalination plant supplies irrigation water at 5 MAD/m³, which is five times more than conventional water. A separate source reports that desalinated water in Agadir costs 8 to 11 dirhams per m³ when plants are powered by fossil energy. Conventional water from ORMVA systems is priced at 0.5 to 0.6 dirham/m³.

The 10 MAD/m³ weighted cost is therefore conservative for a site that relies partly on desalinated water and partly on groundwater. The shadow price of water in Souss-Massa has been estimated at 2.79 to 9.14 MAD/m³ for different crops, and can rise to 15 MAD/m³ under drought conditions.

At 10 MAD/m³, the potential financial saving is

875{,}000 \text{ MAD/year}.

C.4 The \lambda Parameter

The parameter \lambda \in [0,1] represents the fraction of conserved water that translates into cash savings. Its value depends on the water pricing regime:

Regime Description \lambda
Volumetric pricing Farmer pays per m³; reduced consumption reduces the bill 1.0
Quota-constrained Farmer pays a fixed fee for a water allocation; conserved water does not reduce the bill 0.0
Partial volumetric Some water is volumetrically priced, some is quota-allocated 0.0–1.0
