Appendix D: Yield Uplift Derivation

D.1 Baseline Yield

Souss-Massa citrus yields vary by variety and management. The region has approximately 39,000 hectares of citrus, producing 471,550 tonnes in the 2024–2025 campaign. This implies an average yield of

\frac{471{,}550}{39{,}000} \approx 12.1 \text{ tonnes/ha}.

However, this average includes young orchards and low-performing plots. Well-managed export-grade orchards achieve 30 to 40 tonnes/ha. A study of water needs in citrus in a dry region of Morocco reported obtaining 40 tonnes/ha with optimized irrigation. The base case assumes 40 tonnes/ha for the pilot.

D.2 Packout Uplift

The yield uplift of 100,000 MAD/year is derived from a 4% improvement in export packout. If baseline yield is Y = 40 tonnes/ha, the improvement is

\Delta Y = 40 \times 0.04 = 1.6 \text{ tonnes/ha}.

For 50 ha:

\Delta Q = 1.6 \times 50 = 80 \text{ tonnes/year}.

At an export price of 5.38 MAD/kg (5,380 MAD/tonne):

\Delta R_y = 80 \times 5{,}380 = 430{,}400 \text{ MAD/year}.

The base case uses 100,000 MAD, which is significantly more conservative. This is appropriate because:

1. Not all additional yield may meet export grade.
2. Price variability and market access constraints apply.
3. The improvement may be in fruit size and uniformity rather than total tonnes.

The 100,000 MAD figure should be treated as a lower-bound estimate. The actual value will be measured as a secondary endpoint.

D.3 Conditions for Yield Uplift

Yield uplift is expected from:

· Improved soil moisture control: Avoiding both under-irrigation (yield loss) and over-irrigation (root hypoxia, nutrient leaching, deep percolation).
· Reduced deep percolation: The study by Abou Ali et al. (2023) found that 52% of applied water was lost to deep percolation, carrying nutrients below the root zone. Reducing this loss improves nutrient use efficiency.
· Better fruit quality: Consistent moisture reduces fruit cracking and improves size and color.

A study on Moroccan citrus found that irrigation water use efficiency averaged 71.2%, meaning that a 12.8% increase in citrus yield (3.408 tonnes/ha) is possible with current technology. The 4% packout improvement is well within this range.

