Appendix N: Water Scarcity Context

Morocco’s per capita renewable water resources have declined from 2,600 m³/year in 1960 to less than 600 m³/year in 2025, placing the country below the threshold of severe water stress. By 2035, Morocco may face absolute water scarcity, defined as less than 500 m³ per capita per year.

The agricultural sector consumes more than 85% of national water use and has benefited from large-scale programs such as the Green Morocco Plan and Generation Green (2020–2030), which promoted drip irrigation on hundreds of thousands of hectares. However, the Jevons paradox has been observed: water savings from drip irrigation have been used to expand irrigated area and grow water-intensive, high-value crops such as avocado, watermelon, and out-of-season tomatoes. Groundwater levels in the Souss aquifer have declined by more than 1 metre per year, and the Chtouka aquifer has dropped 30 metres between 1970 and 2015.

The Souss-Massa aquifer is critically overexploited, with extraction rates far exceeding natural recharge. Groundwater levels have declined by up to 30 metres in the last 30 years, and the aquifer faces seawater intrusion and water quality degradation. The region is a key agricultural zone, producing citrus, vegetables, and export crops, and is therefore the appropriate site for the first MWTL pilot.

The Chtouka-Aït Baha desalination plant, operational since 2022, supplies water to 12,000 hectares of crops and 1.6 million people, with capacity expected to reach 400,000 m³/day by 2026. The plant has allowed authorities to avoid an estimated annual loss of over 860 million euros and the disappearance of more than a million jobs in the region. However, the desalinated water remains out of reach for small operators due to its high cost of 5 MAD/m³ for irrigation, which is five times more than conventional water.

